          "title": "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"
        },
        "question": {
          ":question/id": "41cad213-eb13-55e2-87e6-f9a2adaf2d2e",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\nx=5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe trigonometric terms have been isolated for you:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{5}=\\cos\\theta,\\qquad \\frac{y}{2}=\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nSquare both equations and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$. Choose the resulting Cartesian equation.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Square the isolated $x$ equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\\\\\\frac{x^2}{25}&=\\cos^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare the isolated $y$ equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\\\\\\frac{y^2}{4}&=\\sin^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the squared equations and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{25}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nThus the Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{25}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThe full parameter interval traces the entire ellipse.",
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/id": "32edbe0f-3142-5a53-a1d0-aff03856aca1",
              ":answer-field/key": "selection",
              ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
